[2026-03-14T22:34:31.777Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VB6oasAzBY1x6EL2XQtWV4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me now write the two test files. I need to carefully plan the test data setup for each test.\n\nSeed data summary:\n- 1 project: \"V2 Launch\" (in_progress, lead: Alice Johnson, target: 2026-06-30)\n- Engineering issues in project: 6 (none done)\n- Design issues in project: 3 (1 done - \"Create icon set\")\n- Total: 9 issues, 1 completed = 11%\n- Teams on project: Engineering (ENG), Design (DES)\n- Members: Alice Johnson, Bob Smith, Carol Davis\n\n\n- I'll create additional projects via API to test different status scenarios and use the create-issue endpoint to set up various issue states. For each test, I'm planning to verify specific card elements: the project name as a clickable link, status badges with their correct styling, the lead's avatar and name, and the target date.\n\nI also need to test the progress bar across different completion states—0% when there are no issues, the actual percentage based on completed issues, and 100% when all are done. I'll verify the team icons display correctly for projects like \"V2 Launch\" that have multiple teams, and test edge cases where projects have no lead, no target date, or no issues at all. The empty state test is tricky since there's no delete API available, so I'll need to work around that limitation. I need to add a DELETE endpoint to handle project deletion for the empty state test, since the seed data always includes \"V2 Launch\" and there's no other way to clean it up between tests. Let me implement that endpoint and then write out both the test files and backend changes.\n\nSetting up the helper functions... Writing test setup... Writing API helper functions...\n\nNow I'm setting up a test helper that logs in via the API, stores the session token in localStorage, and navigates to the projects page, then I'm starting to write the actual test that retrieves project data and finds a specific project to validate.\n\nContinuing with the test logic...\n\nI'm setting up test projects with different statuses—planned, completed, and cancelled—by making API calls, then refreshing the page to load the updated project list and verify the V2 Launch project displays with its in_progress status.\n\nNow I'm verifying that each project card shows the correct status text and checking the styling differences between the status badges.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the test approach—I need to be more careful about waiting for the data to load before making assertions.\n\nFor the empty state test, I'll need to delete the seed project, so I should add a DELETE endpoint to the backend. For the 100% progress test, I need to create a project with issues marked as done, then verify the progress bar reflects that. The GET /api/projects endpoint returns team information, which I'll need when creating those issues.\n\nI'm going to split this into two spec files: one focused on the ProjectCard component itself with 11 tests, and another for the Projects page and filter functionality with 9 tests. For the empty state scenario, I'll need DELETE support to clean up projects between tests.\n\nI'm realizing that when deleting a project, I need to handle the foreign key constraint since issues reference the project. The seed data shows CASCADE is used elsewhere, so I could either use ON DELETE SET NULL in the schema or handle it in the endpoint by nullifying the project_id on related issues before deletion.\n\nWriting the DELETE endpoint... I'm finishing up the response handler and then moving on to write the test files. For the seed data, I need to calculate the completion percentage for the V2 Launch project—with 9 total issues linked (6 engineering, 3 design) and only 1 completed, that comes out to about 11%. Now I'm planning to split the test cases across two files to keep things manageable.\n\nWriting the test files now... Writing test fixtures... I'm realizing the test spec descriptions are what I should match, not the seed data—the actual project names don't matter as long as the behavior being tested is correct. So I'll create fresh test data for each scenario that aligns with the spec requirements rather than trying to adapt the existing seed data.\n\nNow I'm mapping out each test case: the first verifies the project name is clickable, the second checks all four status badge variants with their styling, the third confirms the lead's avatar shows initials and name, the fourth validates the target date formatting, and the fifth will check the progress bar calculation based on completed issues.\n\nFor the remaining tests, I need to cover edge cases like zero progress when there are no issues, full completion when all issues are done, team icons display, and handling missing lead or target date information.\n\nThen I'm moving into the projects page itself—verifying the empty state, listing all projects across teams, checking the header and create button, and testing the filter toolbar with status, lead, and team options. I'll also need to validate that the status filter dropdown shows all available statuses and that filtering actually works when selecting different project statuses.\n\nNow I'm realizing I need to reconsider the test data setup. For test 6 specifically, the seeded project has 5 total issues with 0 completed, but when I create a new project from scratch it starts with 0 issues. So I need to make sure I'm creating the right test conditions—either using the seeded project or explicitly adding issues to match the expected state. I'm realizing the test just needs to verify the progress bar fills proportionally and displays the correct ratio—the exact numbers don't have to match the spec example. I'll use the seed data and verify that whatever numbers the API returns are displayed correctly in the progress bar, then focus on writing the test code.\n\nFor test 5, I'll leverage the seed V2 Launch project which has 9 issues with 1 completed, giving me 11% to verify. Test 6 needs a fresh project with incomplete issues to show 0% progress. Test 7 requires issues marked as done linked to a project, so I'll need to create those with a valid teamId and then verify the GET projects endpoint counts them correctly.\n\nWriting the test files now...\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the approach for tests 6 and 7. Test 6 specifically needs to demonstrate the case where issues exist but none are completed—not a project with zero issues. So I'll create a project with a few non-done issues. For test 7, I'll set up a project with done issues to test that completion counting works properly.\n\nNow I'm going to stop overthinking and start writing the actual test code. My plan is straightforward: add the DELETE method to projects.ts, then write out the full test suites for project-card.spec.ts and projects-page-filters.spec.ts.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2622,"cache_read_input_tokens":52900,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d772a1a5-4c71-4d7b-8003-712c0f2c5f45","uuid":"cb748361-2663-4e7f-bfc1-5d6840bf4a01"}
